  "description": "Researchers discovered that Cretaceous oviraptor dinosaurs may have used both body heat and sunlight to incubate eggs arranged in wide nests.",
  "articleSummary": "A study tested the hypothesis that Cretaceous oviraptors laid their eggs in large, ring‑shaped clutches surrounded by a hollow space where the adult sat, which could not support traditional thermoregulatory contact incubation. To simulate this scenario, researchers built a life‑size oviraptor nest with a model dinosaur and placed resin‑molded eggs in a double‑ring configuration. Temperature measurements showed that in cool environments, eggs in the outer ring varied by up to 11 °F, increasing the risk of hatching timing mismatches, while in warmer conditions the temperature span fell to about 1 °F, suggesting that ambient heat, likely from sunlight, helped equalize temperatures across the clutch.",
